## Interface: 20506
## Category: Commander
## Title: Commander Quest Log
## Author: Devin
## Version: 1.7.0
## Notes: The campaign ledger — one double-wide window over the whole quest log: every quest listed down the left under its zone, searchable and sortable and filterable, with the full briefing on the right — objectives with their progress, the description, every reward with its tooltip — and Track, Share, Announce and Abandon on the same screen. Under the list, two clocks: the daily reset, and the weekly reset that pays arena points and clears raid lockouts. It replaces the stock quest log rather than sitting beside it. With Questie installed, a Zones tab turns it into a completionist's guide: pick a zone, or let it follow you, and see every quest it holds for this character — done, open, locked and why, missed and why — with a completion bar, the chains in order, and what to do next. The world map gets the same zone in a drawer down its right edge, with pins on the quest givers Questie does not draw. Go buttons send the Commander Orders arrow to where a quest starts, to its turn-in, to each objective Questie can place (the creatures, the object, the item's droppers, the spot to explore) and to a follow-up's giver; Shift-click shares the place, Ctrl-click copies its /way line, Ctrl+Right-click on a log row goes to its turn-in, and a quest's waypoint goes when it is turned in or abandoned. A nearest-first route through every open quest in a zone goes to the arrow too. A Reputations tab does the same for a faction: every quest that pays it, what each is worth to you now, what is left, whether quests alone reach the next standing or Exalted, the quests to do before their door shuts, and the repeatables and dailies that carry on from there. And every place a quest is shown — the log, the Zones tab, the map drawer, a waypoint, the quest giver's own window — says what it pays in reputation, "No reputation" included: one number, worked out by the module's own rules (Diplomacy, the Hallow's End buff, the Aldor and Scryers losses, spillover, the quests that stop paying at a standing) and corrected by what the game was seen to pay.
